nodecode-rocketchat is a rewrite of HalfbitStudio's HalfbitStudio/hermes-plugin-rocketchat:

  https://github.com/HalfbitStudio/hermes-plugin-rocketchat
  commit 989a04cd5f09e57bdd88997940cd18e4d58415b2
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 hermes-plugin-rocketchat contributors
  (Nous Research, cyb0rgk1tty, meron1122, HearthCore / Timo Goetzken) (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the plugin's ten model-facing tools - rocketchat_list_channels,
rocketchat_get_history, rocketchat_search_messages, rocketchat_get_thread,
rocketchat_get_permalink, rocketchat_create_channel, rocketchat_post,
rocketchat_send_file, rocketchat_dm and rocketchat_delegate - with their
argument schemas and REST endpoints (channels.list, groups.list, rooms.info,
channels/groups/im.history, chat.search, chat.getMessage,
chat.getThreadMessages, chat.postMessage, im.create, rooms.media and
rooms.mediaConfirm), the compacted message shape they answer with and its
untrusted-content marking, the write-tools and file-upload opt-ins, the room
allowlist, the local-file upload roots and size cap, and the delegation
envelope that keeps two bots from replying to each other forever. The Python
(aiohttp) is Common Lisp here: nlk:http for the server's REST API v1, one
section declaration for the settings, and ten nlk:define-contract
declarations for the tools' arguments, so the schema the model is shown and
the body that runs are one shape.

Left out: the inbound gateway half of the plugin - the Realtime (DDP)
WebSocket subscription, the DDP keepalive and resume, the mention and
free-response gating, reactions, thread-context injection, voice-note
transcription through ffmpeg, and the topic-sync and slash-command
forwarding. That half is how the plugin makes Hermes answer as a bot inside
Rocket.Chat; Nodecode's own chat surfaces are cells of the rooms kind, so a
Rocket.Chat gateway would be a second cell rather than a part of this one.
This cell is the half a session uses from anywhere: the ten tools over the
REST API.
